WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Film Courage: Would you say that 99&nbsp;
percent of all movies today are garbage?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.720
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of&nbsp;
FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:15.200
In an age where most movies are referred to as&nbsp;
content, I would say that 99 percent of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:24.880
movies are pretty…bad. I always try…when I say&nbsp;
99 percent I mean of mainstream. When you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.360
(and I think one of the best examples of this&nbsp;
is the sort of Zack Snyder's Justice League)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:41.440
and how that was bungled. How that was bungled,&nbsp;
how the Star Wars franchise was bungled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:48.400
how they could not…I mean to me I’m the studio&nbsp;
executive in charge of Star wars and we're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.080
a new star wars movie and the first thing I’m&nbsp;
going to do is I’m going to put Han, Luke and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.960
Leia in a scene together and the fact that that&nbsp;
moment we will never be able to have that moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:05.680
in cinema when Carrie Fisher was alive, that to me&nbsp;
is I mean that's that's malpractice, that's studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.320
malpractice. The fact that they had with Zack&nbsp;
Snyder's vision what he was building up to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.320
I think seeing the four-hour final product of&nbsp;
what Zack Snyder's Justice League turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.640
to be and that they made this garbage thing Joss&nbsp;
Whedon I mean it's disheartening to me to see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.680
I will say on an indie level tend to take like a&nbsp;
glass half full view and I tend to always look for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:35.360
the positive when it comes to indie films because&nbsp;
you cannot compare the…you can't compare an indie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:43.920
movie shot in 12 days for $10,000 dollars&nbsp;
to Justice League whichever version you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:50.960
talking about you can't compare because in my&nbsp;
mind there is no excuse for a studio to fail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.640
when they put out a blockbuster movie you have no&nbsp;
excuse. Your job is to entertain me, not lecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:04.400
me. Not lecture me, your job is to entertain me.&nbsp;
Unfortunately we live in a time you know back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.240
in the day when Star Wars was first made I feel&nbsp;
like they were checking one box it was called fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:18.160
and now there's not only there's a million boxes&nbsp;
that must be checked. There are all sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.400
noting and committees and this thing and&nbsp;
this is where you end up with…you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:31.920
that last Star Wars movie by J.J. Abrams is&nbsp;
unwatchable. I mean it's garbage. It's exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.400
it informs exactly everything wrong with the&nbsp;
studio system. It's terrible. So I look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:47.680
like the Justice League debacle, the Star Wars&nbsp;
franchise and how that has been was mishandled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.760
and I look at that and I just think how&nbsp;
could that have been done? Where were the…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:03:00.800
where was the…we they ever like looking at the&nbsp;
fans and what they think? What do the fans think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.640
of this stuff? Are they satisfying the fans? Most&nbsp;
normal people don't care. Is it entertaining?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.920
Taking my family especially how expensive it is&nbsp;
to the movie theater experience which is is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:20.000
away right? The theatrical experience of seeing a&nbsp;
movie is going away. You had better entertain me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.320
and I think that whether Hollywood notices&nbsp;
it or not, I think that people don't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.640
like politics woven into fairly mainstream (which&nbsp;
should be mainstream) entertainment experiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.880
And I think we're seeing that more and more.&nbsp;
It's unbelievable to me. I think you're leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.240
money on the table for not giving what the&nbsp;
customer is looking for in that experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.240
In my mind, studios there's no excuse to&nbsp;
fail. You have the best people in marketing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.800
You have the most talented actors, craftsmen. When&nbsp;
it comes to special effects and cinematography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.440
music - there's no excuse for that to fail&nbsp;
and I think part of the failing of Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.920
is that the marketing is so good. Sometimes I’ve&nbsp;
argued with friends sometimes that I believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.800
there should be a key art…I mean there are key&nbsp;
art awards you know for key movie poster art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.360
I’m a fan of movie poster art. There's a really&nbsp;
good documentary about movie poster art called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.240
24x36: A Movie About Movie Posters [Director&nbsp;
Kevin Burke]. Check out that documentary. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.600
you know there should almost a marketing award and&nbsp;
here's the problem, the conundrum, the marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.560
for some for these movies is so good, the&nbsp;
product doesn't live up to the marketing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.880
I got chills watching the trailers for The Rise&nbsp;
of Skywalker. I was so excited to see that movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.640
The ninth I mean, I was a kid when I saw Star&nbsp;
Wars. I’m seeing the ninth movie in this saga.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:57.840
I cannot believe how horrible it turned out. It’s&nbsp;
really to me, it's malpractice. What happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.400
to that franchise and how it's divided fandom?&nbsp;
Fighting over things that are really irrelevant? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:11.680
think it's well not entirely irrelevant, the fact&nbsp;
that they're just like you're seeing people from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.560
within that company attack the fans, it's just&nbsp;
like it’s…I hate to see it because I’m like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.840
nerds are my people I go to San Diego Comic-Con&nbsp;
every year. I have since the 90’s gone to San&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:28.400
Diego Comic-Con and so to see people fractured&nbsp;
over this is really disheartening. But when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:33.440
comes to studio movies there's no excuse not to&nbsp;
fail. Now when it comes to small indie movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.720
to make a good movie under those circumstances&nbsp;
without the resources, without the money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.960
without the great people in marketing without the&nbsp;
budget, without all of the best of circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:51.120
to make an indie movie that like actually can you&nbsp;
can be touched and moved by that is something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.880
be said. Which is why i've always gravitated&nbsp;
toward indie film. It's a miracle when you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:05.360
something that's watchable out of a studio. But I&nbsp;
don't know like where the executives, I mean other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.520
than like maybe coming to mind like a kevin frose&nbsp;
which you know maybe his best days are behind him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.000
you know I can't think of a studio&nbsp;
executive that is a visionary these days.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.160
Film Courage: Are those the jocks?&nbsp;
The nerds are the consumers?

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:26.080
Chris: Yeah the jocks. I mean when we&nbsp;
look at like the era the 70’s being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.880
last great era of American&nbsp;
filmmaking, we really are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.720
losing that American identity because we’ve&nbsp;
become so fractured as a culture and I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.880
where's the movie that we all love&nbsp;
without debate? That we've all loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.680
exactly? I don't know that there is one and and&nbsp;
that's really, I really at the end of the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:07:01.520
I miss loving Star Wars. I miss loving it. I used&nbsp;
to love it and now i'm saddened by that franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.360
I’m using that as like the best example. There's&nbsp;
a lot of other examples we could talk about The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.080
Terminator franchise, we could talk about Star&nbsp;
Trek, we can talk about these these sort of dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:16.640
franchises that have sort of lost their way. But&nbsp;
when you look at like I just saw a documentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.720
called Laddie: The Man Behind the Movies&nbsp;
[Director Amanda Ladd Jones] about Alan Ladd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:28.160
the visionary studio executive who basically&nbsp;
green lit Star Wars and protected George Lucas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.160
I’m just afraid we're never going to see another&nbsp;
George Lucas right? We're never going to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:38.400
another real creative visionary&nbsp;
that changes the industry because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.360
everything is so corporate. You know it's&nbsp;
like what did Francis Ford Coppola say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.960
factory filmmaking. That's the age in&nbsp;
which we live. There's factory filmmaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.280
which is a product and content and then&nbsp;
there's up-and-coming indie filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.680
and when I talked about checking those boxes&nbsp;
earlier a lot of it is like is it going to appeal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:05.360
international? I mean let's be honest, the box&nbsp;
office internationally and especially in China is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.400
much bigger than the United States. That market,&nbsp;
those dollars…so you can't make a movie for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.640
United States anymore. You have to make a movie&nbsp;
for the world which is fine but I think in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:24.080
that's sort of diluting our identity as a people,&nbsp;
as Americans, as we’ve become more fractured and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:32.800
it's distressing to see. You see it when it's&nbsp;
like I was having a conversation with a friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.600
because of course the movies are always an ongoing&nbsp;
conversation with friends. If you're in my circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.640
it will come up and it's like when you look at&nbsp;
like when certain decisions are organic or when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.560
you see when it's shoe horned in right? When&nbsp;
when certain things are shoe horned in like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.720
say the trope that's become a trope&nbsp;
now, the strong female lead it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.320
Ripley [actress Sigourney Weaver in Alien] was&nbsp;
a great character. Just write a great character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.760
not try to shoehorn in a type right? You&nbsp;
kind of know it when you see it. You go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.200
“Oh this is cliche!” You're trying to shoehorn&nbsp;
in a type rather than like that's just a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.160
character. You're also talking to a guy when I&nbsp;
ever play a video game I always choose a female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.560
character. I don't know why I just do that&nbsp;
as someone who has watched so many movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.680
as someone who has made their own films, why do&nbsp;
you think there's so many bad films that are made?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.560
Well I look, the reason there's so&nbsp;
many bad films these days is I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:41.120
1) the barrier to entry is much easier, but I&nbsp;
also think that you're there on an indie level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.920
I see bad indie films too right? I usually want to&nbsp;
want to help them and say “This was your lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.840
okay you'll make a better movie in&nbsp;
your second movie if you don't quit.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.680
But when it comes to studio films there's just too&nbsp;
many notes. There are too many people that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.800
sort of you know the modern day ball player right?&nbsp;
Just like it's not about the passion for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.920
the game. I don't have a passion of hitting the&nbsp;
ball and hearing the roar of the crowd. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.880
the licensing deals and it's this and the sales&nbsp;
of that and the tv commercials and whatnot. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.800
when I look back at the movie The Player which&nbsp;
came out when I first moved to Los Angeles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.920
Robert Altman's The Player [1992] things haven't&nbsp;
changed. The subject has changed. I mean that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:36.080
sort of, it was erotic thrillers and it was Julia&nbsp;
Roberts and Bruce Willis were being cast in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:42.000
movie that was pitched in that film. Now it's&nbsp;
superhero movies, but it's really too many cooks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:50.320
and I feel like the people being elevated to these&nbsp;
positions are good executives but they're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.560
people that know the material. The heart of the&nbsp;
material and when you look at the history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:02.800
creatives who really created something like a&nbsp;
George Lucas or a Gene Roddenberry things begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:10.640
to lose their way when they get sidelined right?&nbsp;
You really need those. I just feel like the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:17.760
day Hollywood now and especially with&nbsp;
streaming changing the game, theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:25.680
the survival of theaters being in doubt. Really&nbsp;
we may live at a time where maybe the billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.840
dollar blockbuster may not be back for a long&nbsp;
time and that may end up being a good thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:39.200
right? Like let's make…I’ll joke with a friend&nbsp;
and I’ll say my favorite Spider-Man movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:43.840
the one where Mary Jane is in peril at the&nbsp;
end which is pretty much all the Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.480
movies or my favorite science fiction movie is&nbsp;
the movie where that thing blows up at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:55.120
that's every science fiction movie right?&nbsp;
Which is why and The Empire Strikes Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:00.560
with such a because it wasn't about “we got&nbsp;
to do and blow up the thing.” That's boring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:08.400
It’s just creatively bankrupt which is why Empire&nbsp;
Strikes back holds up everybody says “Oh it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:13.840
best Star Wars movie.” I mean you could argue it&nbsp;
but it's yeah because it didn't do that thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:22.880
happened in all the other movies and so trying&nbsp;
to go against doing that thing or fighting a blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:28.960
light, there's a lot of fighting a blue light&nbsp;
that happens in films. Fighting a blue light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:35.840
saving the person at the end, the thing that&nbsp;
blows up at the end and I say defy those cliches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:44.480
at all costs. It’s funny because you know there's&nbsp;
still people that are able to work within studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:52.480
system and deliver worthwhile films, Christopher&nbsp;
Nolan being among them. I’ve quite admired his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:57.280
work although he has a tendency to make things a&nbsp;
little overly complex for no reason just to brag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:02.000
about how smart he is but there's always a point&nbsp;
in his films and you'll notice it once I pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.560
out where a character in a Christopher Nolan movie&nbsp;
will say “We have to go down the thing and grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:11.920
the thing to do this and shoot this.” It's Captain&nbsp;
Narrative right? It's the person that stops the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:17.200
movie to tell you what's happening in the movie&nbsp;
to explain what's going to happen so the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:23.440
(the dumb audience) understands and to me so&nbsp;
you got that studio note and he compromises in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:29.840
a way that doesn't completely destroy what he's&nbsp;
trying to achieve as a filmmaker. So he's willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:35.760
to play ball but I just see and I think the&nbsp;
most recent example being Zack Snyder's Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:42.320
League and the fact that that universe will remain&nbsp;
unexplored unfortunately which is disheartening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:49.040
You look at that and i think part of the reason&nbsp;
that Zack Snyder's Justice League was suppressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:55.280
was because once it came out and you see these&nbsp;
are the choices the studio executives made, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:02.640
decided to hire Joss Whedon, they decided to&nbsp;
change everything about it in exchange for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:08.400
like it makes them look bad right because what&nbsp;
Zack Snyder was trying to do is much much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:16.080
clearly whether you enjoy it personally or not, it&nbsp;
was objectively much better than what Joss Whedon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.680
ended up coming up with which was effectively&nbsp;
a dumb cartoon, a live action dumb cartoon.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:28.080
Film Courage: So what about adaptations? Do you&nbsp;
think that's has that always been around or?

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:29.025
Chris: Adaptations of what?

00:14:29.025 --> 00:14:31.120
Film Courage: Adaptations of popular books.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:35.680
Chris: Not necessarily, doesn't have&nbsp;
to be a franchise but think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:40.800
it's here's what's dead is originality. I&nbsp;
mean people forget what George Lucas created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:45.920
in Star Wars was something completely original.&nbsp;
I mean yes it was influenced by so many things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:55.920
westerns, Flash Gordon and Buck Rogers serials&nbsp;
and whatnot but almost nothing that you see isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.960
(nothing that you see in the studio realm) it's&nbsp;
all based on something, pre-existing material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:07.280
It's a book, a graphic novel, a comic book, a&nbsp;
thing that kind of proved the concept right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:13.600
then…or a franchise right? It's a thing that you&nbsp;
know whether it's Star Wars, Star Trek whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:21.120
comic book characters that have been around for&nbsp;
years, it's very rare that there'll be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:32.640
that's a wholly original concept. It would really&nbsp;
be interesting if studios rolled the dice more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:36.880
to get something like a Joker. What Todd&nbsp;
Phillips did with The Joker right? That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:45.360
Joker film which was a low-budget movie right?&nbsp;
I chatted with him about it. I’m sure you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:51.600
look up quotes Todd Phillips talking about how he&nbsp;
pitched them doing some sort of DC Black Series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:56.880
It would be these sort of one-off films that&nbsp;
would be stories that didn't really fit with any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:02.320
sort of larger continuity that were like smaller&nbsp;
movies we could explore a part of that universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:10.800
and the fact that movie did so well from being&nbsp;
praised from in terms of at award season I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:17.840
proves his concept was the right way to&nbsp;
go and people forget Todd Phillips came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:25.120
from independent film. I knew him back in the day&nbsp;
when he was making documentaries. He famously has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:27.760
that frat house documentary [Frat House directed&nbsp;
byTodd Phillips Andrew Gurland, 1998] that played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:33.360
Sundance and then never was released.&nbsp;
I think you can find it on Youtube.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:33.850
Film Courage: What about Mank?

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:40.640
Chris: What about Mank? Yeah I don't know. How&nbsp;
you make a Mank? Here's the thing I look at Mank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:48.400
and I’m like I love Mank but I went to film school&nbsp;
and I’ve seen Citizen Kane 100 times and I’ve read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:54.960
books about Citizen Kane and one of my favorite&nbsp;
dvd commentaries is the one that Roger Ebert did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.960 --> 00:17:02.800
for Citizen Kane. I know a lot of the backstory&nbsp;
and I feel like it's almost impossible to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:08.000
Mank. You have to watch Citizen Kane. Maybe&nbsp;
watch Citizen Kane first and then watch Mank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:19.200
